{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":20,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"low birth weight\" prematurity preterm) (+ \"child maltreatment\" \"child abuse\" neglect) (+ \"risk factors\" vulnerability caregiver))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"low birth weight\" prematurity preterm) (+ \"child maltreatment\" \"child abuse\" neglect) (+ \"risk factors\" vulnerability caregiver))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_722814896#7_1337775380","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q752","rank":1,"snippet":"end_char\": 6547.000000 , \"headings\": \"Risk and protective factors for child abuse and neglect Risk and protective factors for child abuse and neglect Why discuss risk and protective factors? Definitions The interaction of risk and protective factors Limitations of the research Common risk factors for child abuse and neglect Table 1: Commonly cited <cover>risk factors for child abuse and neglect Common protective factors for child abuse and neglect Table 2: Protective factors for reducing the incidence of child abuse and neglect Practice applications of skills and protective factors Conclusion Additional CFCA readings References Endnotes Authors and Acknowledgements Publication details \" , \"segment\": \"Risk and protective factors also operate differently as children grow (Li, Godinet, & Arnsberger, 2011). For example, one study found that low birth weight </cover>and birth abnormality ceased to be a risk factor for maltreatment after age 1 (Putnam-Hornstein & Needell, 2011). The evidence furthermore suggests that exposure to risk factors is cumulative. That is, the more risk factors in a child's life, the greater the chance that they will experience maltreatment (Begle, Dumas, & Hanson, 2010; MacKenzie, Kotch, & Lee, 2011). Risk factors are often interconnected. "},"time":332},"model_calls":20,"document_judgments":16,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
