{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":22,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":12034,"output_tokens":368,"total_tokens":12402,"cached_tokens":11008,"reasoning_tokens":162},"output_characters":930,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_261468532#12_384606531","relevance":1,"reason":"This page is about a parenting stress measure and notes that parenting stress is associated with insecure attachment in premature infants and with neglect-prevention samples, but it does not directly explain prematurity as a risk factor for child abuse or maltreatment.","learned":"Parenting stress in samples involving premature infants can be linked to insecure attachment, a possible indirect mechanism, but this result is not direct child-abuse evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ premature preterm prematurity) (+ infant baby child) (+ \"difficult to care\" \"hard to care\" \"special needs\" \"medical needs\") (+ abuse maltreatment neglect))","reason":"Target the clearest mechanism found so far: premature infants may have special or difficult care needs that increase caregiver stress and maltreatment risk."}}}
