{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":54,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q783"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q783"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#9_3378520235","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q783","rank":2,"snippet":"with child abuse include: lack of knowledge about child development lack of knowledge about how to care for children insufficient money/income to support the needs of a child substance abuse psychological problems unbalanced family relationships domestic violence single parenting. The risk of child abuse is increased if a child's parents are young and immature, with unrealistic expectations. Parents who are young and immature may lack knowledge about child development and how to take care of children, as well as the cost of raising a child, leading to unrealistic expectations. These parents may become frustrated when their expectations aren't met. Families with <cover>children who are premature, chronically ill, or who have attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder or a mental health disorder are also at a higher risk for child abuse. These children will have more care requirements, </cover>potentially leading to burnout and increasing the risk of abuse.\" , \"start_char\": 4712.000000 , \"title\": \"The red flags of child abuse : Nursing made Incredibly Easy\" , \"url\": \"https://journals.lww.com/nursingmadeincrediblyeasy/Fulltext/2018/03000/The_red_flags_of_child_abuse.8.aspx\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#9_3378520235","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":54,"document_judgments":44,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":54,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q783"},"model_calls":54,"document_judgments":44,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":54,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q783"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q783"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_768237216#11_1614276714","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q783","rank":3,"snippet":"will my family’s life be changed? Having a baby hospitalized after birth is a stressful experience for families. However, despite the long-awaited arrival in the home, stress can increase for  caregivers after discharge from the NICU. Families sometimes feel very attached to,  or dependent on, the NICU care team. They may feel alone or fear that they are  unable to give proper care for the baby at home. This stress can put mothers at  <cover>increased risk of postpartum depression and sometimes the stress can affect how  families and marriages function. Assessing for family functioning, postpartum  depression, and confidence in taking care of the baby at home are important  components of the medical home. If available, the medical home care coordinator or  social worker and the local Early Intervention Part C Program may provide additional support to  the family. Finding support within the special needs community can be helpful for  families.\" , \"start_char\": 5068.000000 , \"title\": \"Medical Home Portal - Premature Infant </cover>Follow-Up\" , \"url\": \"https://www.medicalhomeportal.org/living-with-child/diagnoses-and-conditions---faqs/premature-infant-follow-up\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#10_3378522351","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1591365968#9_3378520235","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":54,"document_judgments":44,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
