{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":56,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":30283,"output_tokens":476,"total_tokens":30759,"cached_tokens":29440,"reasoning_tokens":226},"output_characters":1126,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_930063563#13_2018967566","relevance":2,"reason":"This shaken baby syndrome/inflicted trauma source lists prematurity among infant perinatal risk factors and places it alongside family stressors, postpartum depression, lack of support, and colicky crying. It supports prematurity as a risk factor for abusive head trauma, but this particular segment does not explicitly explain why prematurity itself raises abuse risk.","learned":"Abusive head trauma risk-factor lists include prematurity as an infant/perinatal risk factor, with caregiver stress, lack of support, postpartum depression, and colicky crying as related risk contexts."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"premature child\" \"premature children\" \"premature baby\" \"premature babies\") (+ \"child abuse\" \"child maltreatment\" \"risk of abuse\") (+ \"more care\" burnout stress overwhelmed))","reason":"Use broader natural phrasing around premature children/babies and more care, burnout, stress, or overwhelm to find additional direct explanations beyond already found nursing and MedicineNet sources."}}}
