{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-220058","model_call":58,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"born prematurely\" \"born premature\" \"premature babies\" \"premature infants\") (+ \"child abuse\" \"abuse risk\" \"maltreatment risk\") (+ parents caregivers stress frustration))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"born prematurely\" \"born premature\" \"premature babies\" \"premature infants\") (+ \"child abuse\" \"abuse risk\" \"maltreatment risk\") (+ parents caregivers stress frustration))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_1302925191#5_2659661138","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q787","rank":1,"snippet":"Today Diagnosis of Cerebral Palsy Why diagnosis is Important? Who’s on the diagnosis care team? RESOURCES Diagnosis \" , \"segment\": \"To qualify for these programs, the child must have a formal diagnosis. Without a diagnosis, parents can fall into a limbo pattern. Who’s on the diagnosis care team? The process for diagnosing Cerebral Palsy usually begins with observations made by the child’s primary care physician, usually a pediatrician, and the child’s <cover>parents. There are some exceptions. If a baby is born prematurely, or at a low birth weight, he or she is monitored closely in the neonatal intensive care unit of the hospital from time of birth. In extreme cases of child abuse, </cover>or shaken baby syndrome a pediatric neurologist called to the hospital’s emergency or NICU unit will diagnosis the child’s condition. In the majority of cases the child will attend regular well-baby visits where the pediatrician first uncovers signs of Cerebral Palsy during examination. In some cases, it is the parents who notice symptoms they relay to the child’s doctor during these visits. Developmental delay, abnormal growth charts, impaired muscle tone, and abnormal reflexes are early "},"time":1498},"model_calls":58,"document_judgments":47,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
